WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.063
Detection engineering metrics should support decision-making.

2
00:00:03.063 --> 00:00:13.849
Useful measures may include alert quality, analyst feedback, detection coverage, investigation outcomes, time to tune detections, and reduction in unnecessary noise.

3
00:00:13.849 --> 00:00:16.134
Vanity metrics can distort behavior.

4
00:00:16.134 --> 00:00:24.675
Counting total alerts, dashboards, or rules may reward activity without showing whether the program is helping defenders make better decisions.

5
00:00:24.675 --> 00:00:28.247
Coverage metrics are useful when they are grounded in real scope.

6
00:00:28.247 --> 00:00:38.305
Teams can map detections to relevant threat behaviors, critical systems, data sources, or control objectives, while still noting assumptions and visibility gaps.

7
00:00:38.305 --> 00:00:41.069
Success should be measured by outcomes and learning.

8
00:00:41.069 --> 00:00:50.358
A mature program uses metrics to decide where to invest, which detections to tune, which gaps to close, and how to improve analyst experience.
